WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:06.130
A networking model worth looking into is the
TCP model or Transmission Control Protocol

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:07.370
model.

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:13.199
Unlike the OSI model, which has seven layers,
the TCP model, also known as the TCP/IP model,

00:00:13.199 --> 00:00:18.590
has four layers: the Network Access Layer,
the Internet Layer, the Transport Layer, and

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:20.359
the Application Layer.

00:00:20.359 --> 00:00:25.439
From the top-down, the Application Layer performs
the duties of the Application, Presentation,

00:00:25.439 --> 00:00:27.650
and Session Layers of the OSI model.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:32.570
Thus, this layer is responsible for formatting
data and opening a session with a recipient

00:00:32.570 --> 00:00:33.960
of the data.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:38.320
The Transport Layer in the TCP model does
the same thing as the Transport Layer in the

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:42.250
OSI model, which is data segmentation.

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:47.260
The Internet Layer in the TCP model mirrors
the Network Layer in the OSI model.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:52.160
This layer is responsible for getting data
into packets and addressing those packets.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:58.160
Finally, the Network Access Layer in the TCP
model has the same duties as the Data Link

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.600
and Physical Layers in the OSI model.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:06.980
That is to break the data down into frames,
add a physical address to the frames, break

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:11.400
the data down into bits, and get those bits
transferred over physical media from a source

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:12.270
to a destination.

